        "title": "Feeling more anxious? Researchers may know what’s driving it",
        "source": "NPR Short Wave",
        "source_id": "npr_short_wave",
        "category": "culture",
        "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/11/27/nx-s1-6044623/anxiety-uncertainty-intolerance-smartphone-genz",
        "published_at": "2026-11-27T08:00:00",
        "level": "B2",
        "slot_level": "B2",
        "source_level": "B2",
        "word_count": 102,
        "duration_sec": 657,
        "body_text": null,
        "audio_url": "https://npr.simplecastaudio.com/72da6803-9aa4-4e2e-a973-3bc436f0e9fa/episodes/f81c2e92-6cdd-4427-9cb7-dd5880f568d0/audio/128/default.mp3/default.mp3_ywr3ahjkcgo_d258e8cf06077de3201e11168c1131b3_12126698.mp3?awCollectionId=72da6803-9aa4-4e2e-a973-3bc436f0e9fa&awEpisodeId=f81c2e92-6cdd-4427-9cb7-dd5880f568d0&feed=F0qJcgXZ&t=podcast&e=nx-s1-6044623&p=510351&d=657&size=10524674&hash_redirect=1&x-total-bytes=12126698&x-ais-classified=unclassified&x-access-range=0-1023&listeningSessionID=0CD_382_330__517ecb61f91c58c2c060a97edbdaf4f8df6ac3bf",
        "description": "Younger people are more anxious these days than previous generations, and scientists have speculated all kinds of reasons as to why. A new study pinpoints when anxiety started rising – about 15 years ago. That aligns with when many people started amping up their smartphone use. But what about phones is making people more anxious? The answer may surprise you: Researchers think it may have to do with the way we handle uncertainty. If you liked this episode, check out our episode on the science of healthy screen time . Interested in more science in the news? Email us your question at shortwave@npr.org .",
        "license": "proprietary",
        "attribution": null,
        "truncated": false,
        "guid_hash": "813b2aeda9cd3e386409a8fc5d0a5293226fef81",
        "summary": "Younger people are more anxious these days than previous generations, and scientists have speculated all kinds of reasons as to why. A new study pinpoints when anxiety started rising – about 15 years ago. That aligns with when many people started amping up their smartphone use. But what about phones is making people more anxious? The answer may surprise you: Researchers think it may have to do with the way we handle uncertainty. If you liked this episode, check out our episode on the science of healthy screen time . Interested in more science in the news? Email us your question at shortwave@npr.org .",
